        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
The Periodic Fitness Evaluation with Oxygen Uptake Measurement (PFE-OUM) was performed to demonstrate the capability of crewmembers to perform periodic fitness evaluations (PFE) with continuous oxygen consumption measurements within 14 days after arrival on ISS, and once monthly during routine PFEs. Once the capability of the pulmonary function system (PFS) is verified to then perform PFEs, crewmembers will be able to integrate their monthly PFE with oxygen consumption measurements to fulfill the requirement for cardiovascular fitness evaluations during long-duration space flight.
<P>
This experiment was a collaborative effort between the European Space Agency (ESA) and NASA. A medical requirement performed on the International Space Station Medical Operations called for the performance of a Periodic Fitness Evaluation (PFE) on board the International Space Station (ISS) with oxygen consumption measurements, referred to as OUM. 
<P>
A measurement of aerobic capacity (VO<sub>2</sub>max) reflected the maximum rate that oxygen can be taken up and utilized by the body during exercise. The magnitude of VO<sub>2</sub>max depends on the capacity of the lungs to exchange oxygen between the air and blood in lung capillaries, the capacity of the cardiovascular system to transport oxygen to the muscles, and the muscles' capacity to use oxygen. The highest rate of oxygen uptake and use reflects the upper limit in the ability of the body to supply energy via aerobic metabolism to the active muscles during strenuous exercise and the ability to carry out weight-bearing tasks. Currently, the crew aerobic capacity on-board is only estimated using heart rate and work load while the crew goes through a set exercise protocol. It was assumed that the oxygen consumption per given work load was equivalent to the pre-flight measurements. The validity of this assumption has not been verified with in-flight data. This investigation provided the necessary hardware to measure oxygen uptake with the crewmember breathing through a mouthpiece in the performance of the routine PFE evaluations. <P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Crewmembers routinely performed a ground-based exercise test using metabolic gas analysis before and after space flight. During these tests, OUMs were made and used to estimate the crewmember's aerobic capacity. Measurements of aerobic capacity allowed exercise physiologists and flight doctors to assess crew health and fitness and accurately prescribe exercise countermeasures for use on-board the ISS. 
<P>
While performing this test, the crewmember's heart rate and blood pressure were recorded using the Blood Pressure/Electrocardiograph (BP/ECG). Heart rate data along with the prescribed cycle ergometer workloads, allowed ground personnel to estimate changes in crewmember aerobic capacity by comparing the heart rate response to increases in workload performed during pre-flight evaluations. There were several limitations to this approach. First, a method does not currently exist to verify that the cycle ergometer was correctly calibrated or maintaining calibration over time on orbit. Second, due to the fact that the cycle ergometer aboard the ISS is vibration isolated, which lead to instability between the exercising subject and the cycle, the metabolic cost associated with exercising on the cycle may differ than that measured during cycle ergometer tests performed on the ground. These factors may lead to misinterpretation of the crewmember's changes in aerobic fitness during long-duration space flight. 
<P>
At the time of this experiment, OUMs had not been possible during the PFE because hardware capable of measuring oxygen uptake had not been available aboard the ISS. Now, the NASA/ESA Pulmonary Function System (PFS) provided the ability to perform the required OUMs. This evaluation was performed in two phases. The first phase was used to assess the feasibility of making OUMs during the PFE. The data provided investigators the necessary information to determine if the PFS was able to accurately perform OUMs aboard the ISS within the current crew timelines designated for the PFE. 
<P>
The second phase of this evaluation was used to compare the current methods of estimating ISS crewmember's aerobic fitness with the more direct method of performing OUMs. The data collected during this phase along with data collected during phase one was analyzed using the current method of estimating changes in aerobic fitness (i.e., workload and heart rate observations alone) and direct analysis of the heart rate data in association with OUMs.
<P>
Preflight, crewmembers performed their required medical testing, Cycle Ergometry (MR080L), using nominal metabolic gas analysis hardware. The testing included a maximum cycle ergometry test at approximately 270 days prior to flight and (L-270 days) and a submaximal cycle test at approximately 30-45 days before flight. 
<P>
Post-flight, the crewmember performed the nominal submaximal cycle test 5-7 days following landing and again at 30 days post-flight as part of the medical testing using the nominal metabolic gas analysis hardware. Crewmembers performed the same submaximal cycle exercise test post-flight as performed pre-flight. <P>

The PFS hardware measured the O2 and CO2 concentrations using the Photoacoustic Analyzer Module (PAM) and the ambient humidity, temperature and pressure. During the exercise test, the subject inhaled cabin air and expired through a mouthpiece, and the nostrils were occluded with a nose clip. The fractions of oxygen and carbon dioxide in the expired air was measured by the PFS and used to calculate oxygen consumption, carbon dioxide production and metabolic rate. Blood pressure was measured at designated time intervals throughout the protocol, while ECG was recorded continuously via the BP/ECG. 
<P>
The initial in-flight PFE-OUM session was performed at mission day 14 and then every 30 days after throughout the mission.
<P>
The NASA/ESA Pulmonary Function System (PFS) was one of the major components of the HRF Rack 2 and was launched to International Space Station (ISS) aboard STS-114 in July 2005. The PFS was a facility class research hardware consisting of: 
<P><UL>
<LI>The Photoacoustic Analyzer Module (PAM) developed by ESA. </LI>
<LI>The Pulmonary Function Module (PFM) developed by ESA. </LI>
<LI>The Gas Analyzer System for Metabolic Analysis Physiology (GASMAP) developed by NASA. </LI>
<LI>The Gas Delivery System (GDS) developed by NASA </LI></UL>
<P>
All hardware pertaining to the OUM activity was stored in the HRF Rack 2 and was checked out during Increment 12. Initial use of the system on-board the ISS was performed by an ESA augmentation crewmember in July 2006 during Increment 13. The PAM determined the concentration of seven respired gas components that also contained significant concentrations of nitrogen and water vapor utilizing two photoacoustic analyzers and the oxygen analyzer mixture. 
<P>
The PFM, when operated together with either the Gas Analyzer System for Metabolic Analysis Physiology (GASMAP) or PAM plus the GDS, formed a respiratory facility capable of a wide range of respiratory and cardiovascular measurements including, breath-by-breath measurements, diffusing capacity of the lung, expiratory reserve volume, forced expired spirometry, functional residual capacity, cardiac output, alveolar ventilation, volume of pulmonary capillary blood, and numerous other specialized tests of pulmonary function. It consisted of the Respiratory Valve Unit with associated flow-meters, a re-breathing bag and an electronics unit. The GDS provided special gas mixtures needed for the calibration of the GASMAP and PAM and for the performance of such physiological tests as re-breathing and washout measurements. The GDS supported five canisters of gas mixtures which were permanently presented on-orbit and supported re-supply of these gases. <P>

RESULTS:<BR>
This experiment has concluded on the ISS as a collaborative effort between the ESA and NASA agency. Eight subjects completed two peak cycle ergometer tests and exercise stage through 250 Watts (W). Therefore, the planned comparisons of the dependent variables were conducted at 100, 150, 200 and 250 W.  The oxygen consumption (VO2)   did not differ between devices at 100, 200 and 250 W; however, a statistically significant difference between the devices was observed at the 150 W stage. At this stage, the percent difference in VO2 between the two devices was 5.4%. <P>

In addition, the peak VO2 of the subjects demonstrated a non-significant difference of 3.8% between the two devices, also within the ranges reported in the literature of day-to-day variation in VO2PK.
No differences were seen in expired pulmonary ventilation (VE), even though the systems
compute pulmonary ventilation using differing methods. Both the PFS and ParvoMedics use a
differential pressure pneumotach to measure flow; however, the PFS measures flow on the inspiratory
side, while the ParvoMedics measures flow on the expiratory side.  Additional information can be found in the NASA Technical Report NASA/TP-2007-214756.
        </div>

